'''GreenArrays, Inc.''' is an American semiconductor company founded in 2009 by computer engineer [[Charles H. Moore]] and colleagues.{{cite web |title=About the Company |url=https://www.greenarraychips.com/home/about/index.php |website=GreenArrays |access-date=17 September 2026}} It develops low-power, asynchronous multicomputer chips and associated software tools.{{cite web |title=About the Company |url=https://www.greenarraychips.com/home/about/index.php |website=GreenArrays |access-date=17 September 2026}} Its products use many small, independently operating stack computers on a single integrated circuit.{{cite web |last=McGrath |first=Dylan |title=144 Complete Computers on a Chip for $20 |website=EDN |date=12 October 2011 |url=https://www.edn.com/144-complete-computers-on-a-chip-for-20/ |access-date=17 September 2026}}

==History==

GreenArrays was established on 13 February 2009.{{cite web |title=About the Company |url=https://www.greenarraychips.com/home/about/index.php |website=GreenArrays |access-date=17 September 2026}} Moore, the creator of the [[Forth (programming language)|Forth]] programming language, served as chairman and chief technology officer.{{cite web |title=The People of GreenArrays |url=https://www.greenarraychips.com/home/about/bios.php |website=GreenArrays |access-date=17 September 2026}}

In 2010, the company announced that the GA144, a chip comprising 144 F18A computers in an 18-by-8 array, had been released to production.{{cite web |title=News |url=https://www.greenarraychips.com/home/news/index.php |website=GreenArrays |date=19 October 2010 |access-date=17 September 2026}} Shipments of the production GA144 began in 2011, when GreenArrays also planned an evaluation board containing two GA144 chips.{{cite web |title=News |url=https://www.greenarraychips.com/home/news/index.php |website=GreenArrays |date=8 July 2011 |access-date=17 September 2026}}

==Technology and products==

GreenArrays' chips are based on the F18 family of 18-bit stack computers. The company has described prototype devices including the four-computer GA4, 32-computer GA32, 40-computer GA40, and 144-computer GA144.{{cite web |title=About the Company |url=https://www.greenarraychips.com/home/about/index.php |website=GreenArrays |access-date=17 September 2026}} The GA144 is an asynchronous array of F18A nodes with local memory and neighbour-to-neighbour communication.{{cite thesis |last=Phothilimthana |first=Phitchaya Mangpo |title=Chlorophyll: Synthesis-Aided Compiler for Low-Power Spatial Architectures |type=Master's report |publisher=University of California, Berkeley |date=15 May 2015 |pages=3–4 |url=https://digicoll.lib.berkeley.edu/record/139996/files/EECS-2015-121.pdf |access-date=17 September 2026}}

The company supplied ''arrayForth'', a development environment for GreenArrays chips that included compilers, simulators and a boot-stream generator.{{cite web |title=EVB001 Evaluation Board for G144A12 |url=https://www.greenarraychips.com/home/documents/greg/EVB001-144x2-Rev1.pdf |publisher=GreenArrays |pages=5–6 |access-date=17 September 2026}} Its EVB001 evaluation board paired two GA144 chips with USB and serial interfaces, SRAM, flash storage and a prototyping area.{{cite web |title=EVB001 Evaluation Board for G144A12 |url=https://www.greenarraychips.com/home/documents/greg/EVB001-144x2-Rev1.pdf |publisher=GreenArrays |pages=1–3 |access-date=17 September 2026}}

